WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.330
Ok, so thulium is a very expensive lanthanide.
It’s very expensive

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.999
because it’s very hard to separate it from
its neighbours. The lanthanides

00:00:11.999 --> 00:00:15.179
from the left to right undergo this contraction
where they get smaller

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:19.523
and smaller and over on the right-hand-side
at this point that they are all

00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:25.090
of a very similar size. Now we can separate
them now but it is quite

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.800
difficult to do and therefore quite expensive,
so thulium is not used very

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.079
often. However thulium, as well as being able
to obtain the plus-3

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:39.649
oxidation state, it is also one of those ones
which can obtain the plus-2

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:43.420
oxidation state, which means that chemists
are now beginning to find

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.550
uses for it which they hadn’t expected before.
So you may see the use

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.300
of thulium increase in years to come. Because
it’s very hard to isolate

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.420
because it’s of a very similar size to its
neighbours, it took a guy called

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.579
Charles James, in 1911, a famous 15,000 times
of recrystalization to

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:10.030
isolate thulium in a pure, albeit as a chloride,
but a pure nonetheless,

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.430
compound for the first time.

